some AI agentic solutions. So POSIT has been a cornerstone tool for us for at least the past

00:03:54.800 --> 00:04:04.000
decade, if not longer. So thank you. Thank you. Well, I will go ahead and pass it on to Inger to

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:19.920
get us started. Thank you. I'm going to share my screen here. All right. Thanks so much, Amy. And

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:24.399
thank you everyone for being here. It's great to be with you. So I work in the Office of

00:04:24.399 --> 00:04:30.880
Institutional Research and Analytics at Harvard, which is part of central administration. And our

00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:37.359
office supports decision making and reporting for the university as a whole. But of course, Harvard,

00:04:37.359 --> 00:04:43.519
like other large research universities, is made up of many different schools and colleges, each with

00:04:43.519 --> 00:04:49.920
its own priorities and data needs. So when our central office does a university-wide analysis,

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:54.079
the results may need to be presented differently depending on the audience and what they care about.

00:04:55.040 --> 00:05:00.880
One example, say we have, if we do an analysis of high demand courses. So the business school

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:05.440
is going to care probably about business school courses and less about medical school courses

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:12.720
and vice versa. And then for sensitive data, something like a climate survey report, each

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:18.399
school needs to see only its own results. So the challenge that I want to talk about and how we

00:05:18.399 --> 00:05:24.559
approached it is essentially a role-based permissions puzzle. Like how do we securely

00:05:24.559 --> 00:05:30.799
deliver school-specific views of basically the same analysis? In other words, how can we create

00:05:30.799 --> 00:05:37.119
a single report that kind of knows who the user is when they open it and presents only the data

00:05:37.119 --> 00:05:46.079
that that person needs to see? So we did have a way of doing this, and it just wasn't ideal.

00:05:46.799 --> 00:05:50.799
But just to kind of give you a sense of where we were coming from, we might do something like

00:05:50.799 --> 00:05:57.760
create a PDF and Excel file for each school, upload them to SharePoint, create school-specific

00:05:57.760 --> 00:06:03.679
folders and links, and then get the right link to the right person. And we tracked this all

00:06:03.679 --> 00:06:08.959
in a spreadsheet, and technically it was working and getting the job done, but it was really not

00:06:08.959 --> 00:06:15.519
efficient, and it created opportunities for mistakes like people getting the wrong link and

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:21.279
us getting the dreaded email of somebody saying my link doesn't work. So what we really wanted was

00:06:22.079 --> 00:06:27.679
one report to maintain, one link to share, one pipeline instead of many different reports to

00:06:27.679 --> 00:06:35.279
manage, and a fragmented pipeline. But we also wanted to solve this using tools that we already

00:06:35.279 --> 00:06:39.760
had in our toolbox. We didn't want to have to go shopping for something new if we could help it,

00:06:40.640 --> 00:06:48.079
and we were already using R and Quarto and Shiny to create the data products, and we were

00:06:48.079 --> 00:06:54.880
using Posit Connect to deploy and share them. And the key for this solution that we ended up

00:06:54.880 --> 00:07:01.040
going with turned out to be something that was already available within Shiny, the session

00:07:01.040 --> 00:07:09.279
argument, and more specifically the session groups object. So if you're familiar with Shiny,

00:07:09.279 --> 00:07:14.160
you might have worked with the input and output arguments within that server function.

00:07:15.279 --> 00:07:20.880
So session is an optional argument that you can also pass through, and where you can pass

00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:29.920
information or metadata about the current user session. So when an authenticated user is running

00:07:29.920 --> 00:07:36.640
that app, that information can tell us something about the viewer that we can then use to tailor

00:07:36.640 --> 00:07:44.880
or kind of control what they see or how the app behaves. And there are actually a lot of different

00:07:44.880 --> 00:07:51.040
pieces of metadata that can be passed through that session argument. This is a list of different

00:07:51.040 --> 00:07:56.720
pieces of metadata that are available that you can pass through. The one that really mattered for us

00:07:56.720 --> 00:08:03.600
in our solution was the session groups object, which lets us pass the groups that are associated

00:08:03.600 --> 00:08:08.959
with that logged in user. In the case I was describing, this would be the group that identifies

00:08:08.959 --> 00:08:17.519
the user's school. So that's what allowed us to move from managing multiple school-specific reports

00:08:18.079 --> 00:08:24.880
to maintaining one single Shiny app on Posit Connect, but one that can serve up different

00:08:24.880 --> 00:08:35.359
views based on the school associated with that logged in user. So far I've talked about kind of

00:08:35.359 --> 00:08:41.520
the why and the what of it all, but now I want to focus more on the how. So what are the main

00:08:41.520 --> 00:08:48.000
building blocks behind this approach, and what are the key pieces of code that are making it all work?

00:08:50.640 --> 00:08:55.840
So a first step is creating the groups that are relevant to the project and assigning

00:08:55.840 --> 00:09:01.200
people to those groups. So you can do this directly within Posit Connect, or

00:09:01.760 --> 00:09:07.520
you can manage groups in an external application and then sync that information with Posit Connect.

00:09:07.520 --> 00:09:14.159
And the latter is what we ended up doing because in our particular environment, we already use

00:09:14.159 --> 00:09:19.520
something called Grouper for access governance. So that's where we can create the groups and

00:09:19.520 --> 00:09:27.520
assign people to them. So Grouper then is synced with Posit Connect so that group membership

00:09:28.159 --> 00:09:35.919
information is passed along to Posit Connect, and then Posit Connect has that group information

00:09:35.919 --> 00:09:42.080
and can pass it to the Shiny app through that session groups object. And the app can then use

00:09:42.080 --> 00:09:47.039
that information to determine what each viewer should be seeing when they log in.

00:09:49.760 --> 00:09:53.919
So putting all that together in our particular setup kind of looks like this. We have these

00:09:53.919 --> 00:09:59.039
three main building blocks. First is Grouper, where we create the groups and assign people to

00:09:59.039 --> 00:10:05.760
them. Second is Posit Connect, where we deploy the Shiny app. And third is the Shiny app itself,

00:10:05.760 --> 00:10:12.000
which uses the viewer's group information to kind of customize what they see.

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:19.599
Now, to make this a little more concrete, I'm going to share this sample Shiny app that I made

00:10:19.599 --> 00:10:26.320
using Hogwarts course data that I made up just to kind of keep things simple. And this particular

00:10:27.039 --> 00:10:34.640
app has three parts that are customized based on my group membership. I'm a Gryffindor, so you'll

00:10:34.640 --> 00:10:39.599
see just Gryffindor-specific information here. And those three parts that are customized are the

00:10:39.599 --> 00:10:47.200
greeting, the course table, and the download file. So the same group information is being used in

00:10:47.200 --> 00:10:54.640
three different places. Now, just briefly, I'm going to take a look at kind of what's behind

00:10:54.640 --> 00:11:00.320
the curtain with this code and see what's making it work. The first step, and probably the most

00:11:00.320 --> 00:11:07.919
important step here, is to create a reactive that returns the user's house. So the session group

00:11:07.919 --> 00:11:13.119
object returns actually all of the groups associated with the user, whether or not the group

00:11:13.119 --> 00:11:18.080
is relevant to this particular report. So what's going on here is we're identifying the group that

00:11:18.080 --> 00:11:25.119
applies to that app and then assigning that to this reactive. So once we have that reactive value,

00:11:25.840 --> 00:11:29.440
we can just use it throughout the application.

00:11:32.880 --> 00:11:40.960
So for instance, this opening, this welcome text string, we can kind of plop that reactive value

00:11:40.960 --> 00:11:44.880
in that string and customize what the user sees in that welcome message.

00:11:46.239 --> 00:11:55.280
Second, we can use it as a filter to display courses in this table that only apply to the user's

00:11:55.919 --> 00:12:05.520
house. In this case, Gryffindor houses. Sorry, Gryffindor classes. And then finally, we can use

00:12:05.520 --> 00:12:10.479
that same reactive value to generate a house-specific download. So you don't have to

00:12:10.479 --> 00:12:15.679
necessarily read all this code, but basically what it's doing is it's using the user's house to

00:12:15.679 --> 00:12:25.440
determine which file to download. So that's the basic pattern. We use the viewers group membership

00:12:25.440 --> 00:12:30.960
to create one reactive value, and then we reuse that value throughout the app to control the

00:12:30.960 --> 00:12:37.919
greeting, to filter the data in the data table, and to select the appropriate download file.

00:12:40.320 --> 00:12:45.679
So before and after here, we were going from managing separate files and links for each school,

00:12:45.679 --> 00:12:54.640
and now we have one shiny app that provides each school with its own view. So what is this bias?

00:12:54.640 --> 00:12:58.479
Was it worth it for us? Yes, it was definitely worth it to kind of re-engineer this.

00:12:59.039 --> 00:13:04.880
For one thing, it gives us a single link to manage rather than 10 or 20, which means fewer

00:13:04.880 --> 00:13:11.359
opportunities for distribution errors and less headache overall. It also means a workflow that

00:13:11.359 --> 00:13:17.359
can scale. So for instance, we'll soon be using the same approach to distribute department-specific

00:13:17.359 --> 00:13:24.719
reports to about 50 to 60 departments within a single school. And then finally, everything is in

00:13:24.719 --> 00:13:31.359
one pipeline now. So we're no longer moving 10 PDFs and Excel files over to SharePoint,

00:13:31.359 --> 00:13:37.359
handling permissions there. So it's all in a single pipeline. So the benefit overall is not only

00:13:38.400 --> 00:13:45.679
a simpler workflow for us, but ultimately a more reliable and consistent experience for the campus

00:13:45.679 --> 00:13:52.000
partners that we're sharing these data products with. Thank you.

00:13:56.239 --> 00:13:58.559
And I will pass it over to, I think, Lu.

00:13:58.559 --> 00:14:21.039
Hi, everyone. My name is Lu. I work at Georgetown University in the Office of

00:14:21.039 --> 00:14:27.280
Assessment and Decision Support. Today, I wanted to share how we've transformed

00:14:27.280 --> 00:14:36.640
our workflow to handle data analytics more efficiently. A quick intro of our team. We're

00:14:36.640 --> 00:14:45.039
a small team of eight analysts with different areas of focus covering IR, survey research, BI,

00:14:45.599 --> 00:14:54.000
data science, data management, and course evaluation. And we all directly report to

00:14:54.000 --> 00:15:03.840
the Associate Vice President of Institutional Research. Since the fall of 2023, we have used

00:15:03.840 --> 00:15:10.640
Posit Connect to publish and share data insights with stakeholders across our campus.

00:15:13.200 --> 00:15:21.440
Before we had Posit, we were faced with some challenges, which ultimately drove us to rethink

00:15:21.440 --> 00:15:28.880
how we work. First is staff turnover. When people leave, they take most of the institutional

00:15:28.880 --> 00:15:35.280
contacts and knowledge they kept in their heads. So we realized the need to document things

00:15:35.280 --> 00:15:40.559
directly in code so that our work can remain consistent and reproducible.

00:15:41.599 --> 00:15:48.159
Then there's this increasing demand for more data, more up-to-date dashboards,

00:15:48.880 --> 00:15:57.919
more predictive modeling, and scenario simulators. It quickly became clear that our old workflow,

00:15:58.479 --> 00:16:05.119
which relied heavily on Excel and PowerPoint, just wasn't going to meet those needs.

00:16:06.000 --> 00:16:13.200
Then last but not least, our team handles many ad hoc requests on a daily basis. And a lot of them

00:16:13.679 --> 00:16:18.400
are really the same type of work, just with small variations. So we

00:16:18.400 --> 00:16:22.479
needed a more streamlined approach to handle them more efficiently.

00:16:25.520 --> 00:16:29.840
And how do we adjust those challenges? We come up with a new workflow.

00:16:30.799 --> 00:16:39.840
So this diagram shows how the workflow works. I'm going to start with the box in the top right

00:16:40.559 --> 00:16:47.919
where it says Google Cloud Platform, or GCP. This is where our enterprise data warehouse is built.

00:16:48.880 --> 00:16:53.760
And our PositConnect is also hosted in a virtual machine in GCP.

00:16:54.799 --> 00:16:59.119
So having them in the same environment makes authentication much simpler.

00:17:00.880 --> 00:17:08.719
The actual workflow starts in a local IDE. We create a portal document or a QMD file.

00:17:08.719 --> 00:17:17.359
So a portal document is a notebook where you can combine regular text and executable code within

00:17:17.359 --> 00:17:25.520
the same place. And in that portal file, we write the code to pull data from BigQuery,

00:17:25.520 --> 00:17:31.680
and that is our data warehouse. And we then do the data wrangling and then produce a clean,

00:17:31.680 --> 00:17:40.319
analysis-ready data set. Next, we use an R package called pings to take that cleaned data set

00:17:40.319 --> 00:17:47.839
and publish it to PositConnect, or ping it to PositConnect. So basically, pinging is putting

00:17:47.839 --> 00:17:55.040
the data in a central location where other applications can access it. And then the

00:17:55.040 --> 00:18:03.199
middle layer is where the automation takes place. We publish the portal file to PositConnect and set

00:18:03.199 --> 00:18:12.400
a schedule. It can be every day, every Sunday, or whatever cadence makes sense. PositConnect then

00:18:12.400 --> 00:18:19.439
runs the code automatically on that schedule. So every time it runs, it pulls fresh data from

00:18:19.439 --> 00:18:26.400
BigQuery, cleans it, and then updates the pings. Everything's automatic, so no one has to do any

00:18:26.400 --> 00:18:37.520
manual work. Then the bottom layer is where we have user-facing applications or data

00:18:37.520 --> 00:18:45.119
products. So anything downstream here that uses those pinged data sets gets updated automatically.

00:18:45.119 --> 00:18:54.719
So dashboards, reports, they all get updated. Everything is in sync. I know there are a lot of

00:18:54.719 --> 00:19:04.959
boxes and arrows here, but the underlying code is actually very straightforward. So here is a

00:19:05.599 --> 00:19:14.959
sample QMD file that handles the authentication and also sets up the pings. So this

00:19:15.760 --> 00:19:23.359
block shows the actual authentication code to establish a secure connection between PositConnect

00:19:23.359 --> 00:19:30.160
and BigQuery. So once that connection is established, the rest of the code can pull the data

00:19:30.160 --> 00:19:37.280
from BigQuery without anyone having to manually log in or provide credentials. And this is

00:19:37.280 --> 00:19:43.199
what allows this automated workflow to securely access the data and run on its own.

00:19:45.520 --> 00:19:52.079
And here is the actual pipeline. At the top here, we're just pulling

00:19:52.880 --> 00:19:56.319
the data from BigQuery using regular SQL queries.

00:19:57.680 --> 00:20:03.760
Then at the bottom, we take that cleaned up data and pin it to PositConnect.

00:20:03.760 --> 00:20:06.400
And this is really the whole pipeline.

00:20:09.760 --> 00:20:15.599
And once we have this automated data pipeline in place, that's where our team can get really

00:20:15.599 --> 00:20:22.880
creative. So we've built all kinds of products on top of it, dashboards, simulators, report

00:20:22.880 --> 00:20:30.400
surveys, and many more. So one pinged data set can serve multiple downstream applications. And then

00:20:30.400 --> 00:20:34.160
when the data updates, everything downstream updates automatically.

00:20:35.280 --> 00:20:40.079
So now I want to show what this looks like in practice with a few examples.

00:20:40.959 --> 00:20:47.439
So here's an example of a dashboard analyzing some synthetic iPads data.

00:20:50.560 --> 00:20:58.239
This is a scenario simulator where users can set different parameters on the left side

00:20:58.239 --> 00:21:07.920
to project seed capacity for a new campus. This is a static report on course evaluation results

00:21:08.479 --> 00:21:14.319
with searchable and sortable columns. So department chairs can quickly find their

00:21:14.319 --> 00:21:18.719
schools' data without needing to submit an ad hoc request.

00:21:21.119 --> 00:21:28.560
And this is a survey report of a very long questionnaire with over 100 or so questions.

00:21:29.280 --> 00:21:34.880
This report was built in Cordo with very detailed analysis that is searchable.

00:21:34.959 --> 00:21:40.239
And also, this makes things very easy to reproduce for future years.

00:21:42.800 --> 00:21:50.959
So to bring everything together, this workflow gives us a couple of benefits. First,

00:21:50.959 --> 00:21:57.359
it gives us a workflow that is documented, reproducible, and even version controlled.

00:21:57.359 --> 00:22:03.280
So the knowledge is now in the code rather than sitting in someone's head or Excel files

00:22:03.359 --> 00:22:11.599
or PowerPoint stack. Second, it gives us scalability. We can build the data pipeline

00:22:11.599 --> 00:22:17.760
once and use it to support many different products downstream without having to start

00:22:17.760 --> 00:22:25.119
from scratch every time. And finally, it makes us much more efficient with ad hoc requests.

00:22:25.119 --> 00:22:31.839
Instead of recreating similar analysis over and over again, we can now often point the

00:22:32.800 --> 00:22:37.439
requesters to an existing data product that already gives them what they need.

00:22:40.640 --> 00:22:44.719
Thank you for your time. I'm happy to take questions during the Q&A.

00:22:44.719 --> 00:22:46.719
Now I'm going to pass it over to Jason.

00:22:52.079 --> 00:22:59.839
Hello, everyone. So thank you to Inger and Lou. I've enjoyed your presentations. I made a note

00:22:59.839 --> 00:23:08.560
about session groups. I'm going to try that out. Okay. So I, again, I'm the Senior Director of

00:23:08.560 --> 00:23:14.560
Advancement Analytics here at the University of Pennsylvania. Today, I'd like to take you

00:23:14.560 --> 00:23:20.479
through how we've been using Posit here at Penn, what our environment looks like, how we've built

00:23:20.479 --> 00:23:25.280
our platform, and some of the exciting things we've been able to accomplish as a result of

00:23:25.280 --> 00:23:32.079
using Posit. So I hope you come away with a clearer picture of how Posit can support analytics

00:23:32.079 --> 00:23:41.760
in a complex higher education setting. The challenge for us, as I think for a lot of you, is

00:23:42.319 --> 00:23:47.599
we operate in a highly complex data environment. So we work across multiple systems. They don't

00:23:48.239 --> 00:23:54.000
always talk to each other very well. We've got academic data, financial data, and of course,

00:23:54.400 --> 00:24:01.280
our own advancement data. So the siloed nature of the systems creates challenges. At the same time,

00:24:01.280 --> 00:24:07.040
we are expected to move quickly, of course, to deliver results. And we also want to find space

00:24:07.040 --> 00:24:14.479
to innovate within our projects. And layered on top of that is our institutional data policies

00:24:16.000 --> 00:24:21.359
and our responsibility to keep our data secure, of course. So Posit really meets all of those

00:24:21.359 --> 00:24:31.280
demands for us. So the tools we're using here, a lot like Ingrid Liu, we're using Posit Connect.

00:24:31.280 --> 00:24:37.040
We use Posit Workbench for our development work. We have Posit Workbench and Connect running on

00:24:37.040 --> 00:24:45.040
Linux on-premises servers here that we administer. We also have recently a Posit team on Snowflake

00:24:45.040 --> 00:24:50.079
Snowpark container services. So just as of January this year, so nine months.

00:24:51.359 --> 00:24:54.959
I'll dig more into what we're doing with that tool a little bit later.

00:24:58.000 --> 00:25:03.920
So again, why it matters, what benefits do we get? Well, we get speed.

