00:34:43.823 --> 00:34:50.622
first of all, what is Commons? Commons is a framework for building self-service data analysis

00:34:50.622 --> 00:34:58.943
agents in R and Python. The package is built on Elmer, which if any of you know what Elmer is,

00:34:58.943 --> 00:35:08.462
Elmer is an R package that connects your programs to LLMs. Chatless is a similar package,

00:35:08.462 --> 00:35:18.063
but in Python. And then ShinyChat is what displays the connections that Elmer and Chatless make

00:35:18.063 --> 00:35:22.543
to a Shiny application. And I'm going to show you what those look like in just a little bit.

00:35:23.742 --> 00:35:32.702
So, those are our open source LLMs stack. Behind Elmer and Chatless, you can use whatever LLM you

00:35:32.702 --> 00:35:40.223
want, including locally developed LLMs, as long as they're written to the open AI standard, which

00:35:40.223 --> 00:35:47.422
almost all of them are. So, why did we do this? So, there are two ways that AI is currently failing

00:35:47.422 --> 00:35:53.983
resource teams. First of all, they're either too locked down that they're very narrow,

00:35:53.983 --> 00:36:00.622
they're very prescribed, it only answers certain highly regulated questions,

00:36:01.182 --> 00:36:07.503
or they're completely untrustworthy, where you just release Clod or Gemini or ChatGPT

00:36:08.063 --> 00:36:12.383
on your data, and you have no idea what they're doing, how they're doing it, or why they're doing

00:36:12.383 --> 00:36:20.622
it. So, Commons takes a third path. We have the flexibility of an agent anchored to the validity

00:36:20.622 --> 00:36:29.823
of the code your team already trusts and the definitions you already abide by. So, how it

00:36:29.823 --> 00:36:36.783
works. So, the user, starting from the left, the user asks a question. Those can be researchers,

00:36:36.783 --> 00:36:43.103
PIs, deans, vice presidents, board members, whomever you want to have ask a question.

00:36:43.742 --> 00:36:51.022
What Commons then does is search their trusted calculations. Those trusted calculations,

00:36:51.022 --> 00:36:59.502
that semantic layer, you create in code. So, it's a, if you think about a Shiny application

00:36:59.502 --> 00:37:07.183
where you have the server UI and the app.UI, you have a document that explains exactly your

00:37:07.183 --> 00:37:16.142
trusted calculations. If a user asks a trusted calculation question, Commons will find it,

00:37:16.142 --> 00:37:22.863
run the code, and then give them the answer, along with a little check mark saying this is a verified

00:37:23.502 --> 00:37:30.623
calculation and you can trust this. Now, in the low trust area, you've got two different

00:37:30.623 --> 00:37:39.423
possibilities. One, you have a cited calculation, which means it didn't find a calculation

00:37:39.423 --> 00:37:49.343
specifically, but in your documentation, it found a description of what a calculation might be.

00:37:49.423 --> 00:37:54.303
And in that case, it'll run the calculation for you, give you the answer, and then it will give

00:37:54.303 --> 00:38:03.582
you a cited tag. And then the last step is if it finds nothing having to do with either a trusted

00:38:03.582 --> 00:38:12.303
calculation or a citing, it'll still run a traditional AI code, but it'll give you a little

00:38:12.303 --> 00:38:18.062
note saying, hey, this is untrusted. You really need to research this before you use it for

00:38:18.062 --> 00:38:25.822
anything of interest. So, how does it work? So, first of all, you have your client, any LLM

00:38:26.543 --> 00:38:35.502
via Elmer or Chatless if you're using Python. So, AWS Bedrock, Anthropic, Azure, GitHub Copilot,

00:38:35.502 --> 00:38:41.423
whatever LLM you want to point it to. Then you have your data sources, anything you can connect

00:38:41.423 --> 00:38:48.623
to, whether it's any kind of DBI connection, Databricks, Snowflake, anything you can connect

00:38:48.623 --> 00:38:56.382
to regularly using Python or R, you can connect to a commons agent. Then, as I mentioned earlier,

00:38:56.382 --> 00:39:01.902
you have your semantic layer, validated R functions or Python functions tagged with an

00:39:01.902 --> 00:39:09.663
at measure tag. This is your trusted code that you have used and have agreed upon.

00:39:09.663 --> 00:39:15.582
Then you have your context layer. This is traditionally what you would have an MCP

00:39:15.582 --> 00:39:21.423
server for, a model context protocol. And then you can also have instructions that you create

00:39:21.983 --> 00:39:27.502
as well. And then that runs through commons, and then you get your governed agent. So,

00:39:27.502 --> 00:39:36.303
let's see what this looks like in action. So, I have a very simple application here. This is

00:39:36.303 --> 00:39:43.262
